goen's fonts, and where they came from
======================================

Two families, 110 woff2 files, 4.12 MiB. They are the files Google Fonts
serves, fetched once and vendored here. Nothing subsets them at build time and
nothing fetches them at run time: `go:embed` puts them in the binary and
assets/assets.go serves them from /static with a digest in the URL and a year
of immutable caching.

Licence
-------

Both are under the SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1, which requires the
licence and the copyright notice to travel with the font files wherever they
are redistributed. Each family directory carries its own OFL.txt, copied
verbatim from that family's directory in github.com/google/fonts, and those
files are embedded and served alongside the fonts rather than only living in
the repository:

  assets/fonts/noto-sans-tc/OFL.txt
    Copyright 2014-2021 Adobe (http://www.adobe.com/),
    with Reserved Font Name 'Source'
  assets/fonts/instrument-sans/OFL.txt
    Copyright 2022 The Instrument Sans Project Authors
    (https://github.com/Instrument/instrument-sans)

The two texts are the same licence; they differ only in the copyright line and
in where the upstream wrapped it. Each is kept as its upstream ships it rather
than merged into one, so a diff against upstream is a diff and not an argument
about formatting. TestEveryFontFamilyCarriesItsLicence fails if a family is
ever added without one.

Nothing here is modified. The OFL permits bundling and redistribution of
unmodified files under the original names; goen changes no glyph, no name and
no metadata.


What is here, and why only this
-------------------------------

  Noto Sans TC     108 files   Han. Every subset Google splits the family into.
  Instrument Sans    2 files   latin and latin-ext.

Source and version, as fonts.gstatic.com paths record them:

  Noto Sans TC     /s/notosanstc/v40/
  Instrument Sans  /s/instrumentsans/v4/

Google revises a family under a new vN path, so a future fetch that lands on a
different version is a deliberate upgrade with a new set of digests, not a
silent drift.

Both are VARIABLE fonts, so one file per subset covers every weight goen
paints. That is why there are 108 Noto Sans TC files and not 432: the static
build needs one file per subset per weight.

The weight a browser can draw is the range fonts.css declares, clamped to the
range of the file's own wght axis. Google's CSS declares 350-600 for Noto Sans
TC and 400-600 for Instrument Sans, and fonts.css copies those. The axes
themselves are wider, read with fontTools' fvar table on the files as vendored:

  Noto Sans TC     wght 100-900   every one of the 108 files
  Instrument Sans  wght 400-700   both files

The 350 goen sets figures in lies inside both axes, and
TestFontStylesheetMatchesTheEmbeddedFonts holds the declared ranges.

Instrument Sans keeps only its Latin subsets. Google serves it in no other
script, and a character outside the ranges here falls to Noto Sans TC and then
the system stack.

Noto Sans TC keeps all 108, including its Latin ones. A browser downloads a
subset only when it paints a character inside that subset's unicode-range, so
the count costs binary size and not bandwidth: a page of Latin text fetches one
file and a page of Han fetches the handful its characters fall in.

Inter and JetBrains Mono are gone: one sans stack serves the storefront and the
back office, and <code> uses the system monospace stack. Newsreader and Space
Grotesk are not here either; a browser on a goen page has never downloaded
either.

How to regenerate
-----------------

Google serves a different woff2 when the family is revised, and its CSS is the
only place the unicode-ranges are published. To take a new revision:

  1. Fetch the two stylesheets with a browser's User-Agent, which is what makes
     Google answer with woff2 rather than ttf:

       curl -A "$BROWSER_UA" \
         'https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Noto+Sans+TC:wght@350..600&display=swap'
       curl -A "$BROWSER_UA" \
         'https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Instrument+Sans:wght@400..600&display=swap'

  2. For each @font-face, download the woff2 it names into the directory for
     its family, named after the subset comment above it (the unlabelled Noto
     Sans TC subsets are numbered cjk-000 upward, in the order Google lists
     them — the order is the unicode-range order and it is stable).

  3. Rewrite assets/css/app/fonts.css from the same stylesheets: one @font-face
     per file, carrying Google's own font-weight, font-style, font-stretch and
     unicode-range, `font-display: swap`, and a src of
     /static/fonts/<family>/<subset>.woff2?v=<first 12 hex of the file's sha256>.

  4. `go test ./assets/` — TestFontStylesheetMatchesTheEmbeddedFonts compares
     every digest in the sheet against the embedded file and fails on a file
     that no rule names.

Subsetting tool and version: NONE, and that is the point rather than an
omission. Every file here is a byte-for-byte copy of what fonts.gstatic.com
serves; Google runs the subsetting and publishes the resulting unicode-ranges
in its CSS, and copying both is the whole step. No fonttools, no pyftsubset, no
build step, nothing added to go.mod or the Makefile, and no way for a local
tool's version to change what a visitor is sent. (The axis ranges above were
read once with fontTools in a throwaway virtualenv outside the repository.)

Fetched with Chrome 153's User-Agent on 2026-10-06 from fonts.googleapis.com
and fonts.gstatic.com. The User-Agent matters: Google answers an older one with
ttf and a different set of subsets.

